course: dictionary
lesson: 6390
title: What is a multivitamin?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A multivitamin is a tablet, gummy or liquid that holds many different vitamins together, and often minerals too. People take one to help them get vitamins they might not get enough of from their food.
explain: Doctors say most vitamins should come from healthy food. A multivitamin is an extra, not a replacement for meals. «A multivitamin can't make up for a poor diet, because food gives fibre, energy and many substances a pill does not contain.» Context tells you that a pill cannot do everything food does.
explain: Sometimes a doctor suggests a multivitamin for a child who is a very fussy eater. «Very fussy eaters may miss some nutrients, so a doctor may suggest a multivitamin while the family widens the diet.» The word while shows that the pill is a short-term help, and the main goal is eating a wider range of food.
fact: A multivitamin is a tablet, gummy or liquid with many vitamins, and often minerals, in one product.
fact: People take a multivitamin to help them get vitamins they might not get enough of from food.
fact: Vitamins and minerals are called nutrients, and most of them should come from a balanced diet.
fact: «Take a multivitamin» means swallow a tablet or gummy, or drink the liquid, as a daily extra.
fact: A «children's multivitamin» is made for young people, so it is made in amounts suited to them.
exercise: «A multivitamin can't make up for a poor diet.» Which of these is a multivitamin? | A bar of chocolate with nuts and raisins | A glass of orange juice made from fruit | *A tablet or gummy with many vitamins in it | A slice of wholemeal bread
exercise: «Very fussy eaters may miss some nutrients, so a doctor may suggest a multivitamin.» Why might a doctor suggest one? | To replace all the meals in a day | To stop the child ever feeling hungry again | *To help a fussy eater get vitamins they might miss | To make the child grow taller overnight
exercise: Doctors say most vitamins should come from what? | Sweets and fizzy drinks | Vitamins in the bath | *Healthy food | Pills and tablets only
test: «A multivitamin can't make up for a poor diet, because food gives fibre, energy and many substances a pill does not contain.» Can a multivitamin replace healthy food? | Only if you are a fussy eater | Yes, if you take it every single day for a year | Yes, a pill can replace every meal of the day | *No, food gives fibre, energy and many things a pill does not
test: What does a multivitamin contain? | Fibre from raw vegetables only | Only one vitamin, taken alone | Sugar and water | *Many different vitamins, and often minerals, together
exam: «Fussy eating usually improves with age.» What does this sentence suggest? | *Fussy eaters often become less fussy as they grow older | Fussy eating only happens in adults | Fussy eating gets worse every year | Fussy eaters never change how they eat
